course: biology
lesson: 391
title: Do animals dream? Twitching dogs, replaying rats and colour-changing octopuses
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Your dog is fast asleep on the rug, but its paws are paddling, its lips twitch and it gives tiny muffled woofs. It looks as if it is running somewhere in its sleep.
explain: People and many animals sleep in stages. In one stage the brain is very busy, the eyes flick about under the lids and the body twitches. In people this is the stage when most vivid dreams happen.
explain: Dogs, cats, rats and birds have this busy stage too, and scientists have even watched sleeping rats replay a maze in their brains. But we cannot ask an animal what it saw, so whether animals dream like us is a careful guess, not a fact.
explain: Scientists look for clues instead: brain patterns, moving eyes, twitching legs and changing colours. The clues are exciting, but the honest answer is: maybe, and we are still finding out.
fact: Dogs and cats have the same sleep stages as people, including the busy stage linked to dreaming, when the brain is active and the eyes move under the lids.
fact: During this busy sleep a part of the brain stops the body acting out the dream, so a sleeping dog or cat only twitches, paddles or makes small sounds.
fact: Kittens and puppies spend even more time in this busy sleep than grown-up cats and dogs.
fact: Scientists recorded rats' brains while they ran a maze and saw the same patterns again while the rats slept; this replay may help the brain store what was learned, but whether the rat experiences it as a dream is still argued.
fact: Scientists reported that the brains of sleeping young zebra finches fired in the same pattern as when they sang, so sleep may help songbirds practise their song.
fact: Octopuses have a quiet sleep and a short active sleep, when their colours flash and their arms twitch; some scientists think this might be a bit like dreaming, but it is still argued.
fact: In 2022 scientists reported that baby jumping spiders resting at night move the eye tubes inside their heads and twitch their legs, a bit like the dreaming stage in mammals; whether spiders dream is an open question.
fact: Scientists reported that bearded dragons switch between two sleep stages again and again through the night, which may mean these stages are very old, though some scientists think reptile sleep works differently.
fact: The platypus, an odd old mammal, was reported to have a large amount of the sleep stage linked to dreaming, but its version looks a bit different from ours and scientists still argue about it.
fact: People go through sleep cycles of about an hour and a half, about four or five a night, each ending in dreaming sleep, which gets longer towards morning.
fact: Most people dream every night even if they forget their dreams, and you remember a dream best if you wake in the middle of it.
fact: People also learn new skills better after a good night's sleep.
fact: Vets say it is better not to touch a twitching, sleeping dog, because a startled dog may snap; let it wake on its own or call its name softly from a distance.
exercise: What is the dog most likely doing when it paddles its legs and woofs softly in its sleep? | It is in the busy sleep stage linked to dreaming | It is having a fit and needs a bowl of cold water at once | It is only pretending to sleep so that it can fool you | It is cold and trying to get warm by running in place
exercise: What happens to the eyes during the busy dreaming stage of sleep? | They move quickly under the closed lids | They open wide and stare at the ceiling | They stop moving completely until morning | They turn white for a few minutes at a time
exercise: Why does a dreaming cat only twitch instead of running around? | A part of the brain stops the body acting out the dream | Cats are far too lazy to bother moving at all in their sleep | Its legs fall asleep a long time before its brain does | It only ever dreams about lying still in the sun
exercise: Who spends more time in busy dreaming sleep? | Kittens more than grown-up cats | Grown-up cats much more than little kittens | Old cats more than any other cats | All cats the very same amount each night
exercise: What did scientists see in sleeping rats that had run a maze? | The same brain patterns as when they ran the maze | The rats getting up and running round the maze in their sleep | The rats forgetting the whole maze by the morning | No brain activity at all while they slept
exercise: Do we know for sure that the rats dream about the maze? | No, whether they experience it as a dream is still argued | Yes, the rats showed it clearly by moving their paws | Yes, scientists watched the whole dream on a computer screen | No, because rats never actually sleep at all
exercise: What did scientists report about sleeping young zebra finches? | Their brains fired in the same pattern as when they sang | They sang their songs out loud all night long without waking | They forgot their songs every night and learned them again | They learned new songs only while they were awake
exercise: What does a sleeping octopus do in its short active sleep? | Its colours flash and its arms twitch | It swims round and round the tank very fast | It goes completely white and keeps very still | It hides all its arms away inside its body
exercise: What did scientists report about baby jumping spiders in 2022? | Their eye tubes moved inside their heads during night rest | They kept spinning neat new webs all night while they were fast asleep | They never rested at all, not even for a minute | They dreamed about flies and the scientists could tell
exercise: What did scientists report about bearded dragons at night? | They switch between two sleep stages again and again | They stay wide awake all night long so they can hunt | They sleep only in one deep stage the whole night | They sleep standing up, balanced on their tails
exercise: Why is the platypus interesting to sleep scientists? | It is an odd old mammal with a lot of dream-like sleep | It never sleeps even once in its whole long life, day or night | It sleeps only under the water for months at a time | It is the only animal on Earth that has dreams
exercise: About how many dreaming stages does a person have in one night? | About four or five | Exactly one, at the end | About twenty | None, only children dream
exercise: How long is one sleep cycle in a person? | About an hour and a half | About five minutes | About six hours | About a whole day
exercise: When do you remember a dream best? | When you wake up in the middle of it | When you sleep very deeply afterwards | When you dream it twice | Never, dreams cannot be remembered
exercise: Why is it so hard to know whether an animal dreams? | We cannot ask it what it saw in its sleep | Animals sleep much too fast for anyone to watch | Animals refuse to sleep anywhere near scientists | Animals forget their dreams on purpose
exercise: Your dog is twitching and whimpering in its sleep. What is safest? | Let it wake on its own or call its name softly from a distance | Shake it hard by the shoulders so that it wakes up as quickly as it can | Pick it up and hug it tightly until it stops whimpering | Pour a little cold water on it to calm it down
test: «my dog's chasing rabbits in his sleep, he told me lol» What is the honest answer? | He may be dreaming, but we can only guess what about | Dogs really can tell their owners what they dreamed about, by barking | Dogs never dream, they are just scratching an itch | He is wide awake and just pretending to be asleep
test: «cats don't dream, they're just thick and twitchy» What is the best correction? | Cats have the same busy dreaming stage of sleep as people | Right, cats have no sleep stages at all, they just doze lightly all day | Right, only dogs can dream and cats are just twitchy | Cats only ever dream when they are wide awake
test: What would happen if a sleeping cat's brain did not stop its body during dreaming sleep? | It might jump up and act out its dream, chasing things that are not there | It would sleep more quietly and more deeply than it ever had before | It would stop dreaming at once and never dream again | Nothing at all would change, because cats never move when asleep
test: Mia reckons that sleep is just wasted time for the brain. Which finding shows she is wrong? | Sleeping rats replayed the maze they had learned that day | Rats forget everything they learned as soon as they sleep | Rats only ever go to sleep when they are bored | Rats can learn their way round mazes only in the dark
test: «spiders dream, it's proven now» What is the most accurate reply? | Jumping spiders showed dream-like eye movements, but whether they dream is still open | Yes, it is fully proven, because the spiders acted out their dreams for the scientists | No, spiders never rest at all, so they cannot possibly dream | Yes, and spiders dream far more than any other animal
test: In a cartoon, an octopus sleeps in a little bed with a nightcap and a smile. What do real octopuses do in active sleep? | Flash colours and twitch their arms | Snore loudly through all eight arms | Sleep upside down inside empty shells | Close their eyes with long eyelashes
test: A sleeping octopus suddenly shows the same colours it shows when hunting. What might this mean? | Some scientists think it might be a bit like dreaming, but it is argued | It has definitely caught a crab in its sleep and is eating it right now in the dark | It is very ill and needs to see a vet straight away | It is really awake and just showing off its colours
test: «went to bed at 10, up at 7, how many sleep cycles roughly» About how many cycles of an hour and a half fit in 9 hours? | About six | About two | About twelve | About nine
test: «i never dream, never ever» What is most likely true? | You probably dream every night but forget your dreams | You are right, because some people never sleep at all | Only old people have dreams, so you will dream later | Dreams only happen to people when they are ill
test: Count them: 3 dogs are sleeping and twitching in a basket. How many legs could be paddling? | 12 | 6 | 9 | 16
test: Why would scientists study lizards and the platypus to learn about our own sleep? | Old kinds of animals may show how sleep stages began long ago | Lizards sleep in exactly the same way as human teenagers do | The platypus is the closest living relative of human beings | Lizards are much easier to wake up than people
test: «my brother shouted at the dog and poked it while it was twitching, it snapped at him» Why did that happen? | A dog startled out of deep sleep may snap without meaning to | The dog was dreaming about biting him and did it for real | Dogs hate people all the time and snap whenever they can | The dog was never asleep at all, it was just waiting
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the present state of knowledge regarding dreaming in non-human animals? | Many animals exhibit a sleep stage associated with dreaming, but whether they dream cannot at present be ascertained | It has been conclusively demonstrated by experiment that all animals dream in precisely the same manner as human beings | It has been conclusively demonstrated that no animal other than man dreams | Dreaming has been observed exclusively in reptiles and in no other class of animal
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a sleeping domestic cat merely twitches during the stage of sleep associated with dreaming. | A region of the brain inhibits the movement of the body during that stage | The muscles of the cat are wholly and permanently paralysed throughout all stages of sleep | The cat is in fact fully awake throughout that stage | The cat possesses no dreaming stage of sleep whatsoever
exam: It is given that a human being passes through sleep cycles each of approximately one and a half hours. Ascertain the approximate number of dreaming stages in one night of normal sleep. | Four or five | One | Fifteen | None whatsoever
exam: Select the response consistent with the established findings in respect of rats which had previously traversed a maze. | Patterns of brain activity recorded during the maze were observed again during subsequent sleep | The rats were observed to run the entire maze physically whilst remaining asleep throughout | All memory of the maze was erased during the subsequent sleep | No brain activity whatsoever was recorded during sleep
